- # Tasks
  - This change depends on `mcp-session-access`, the [session handoff spec]([[Spec: MCP token handoff]]). It needs three things from it:
    - the handoff;
    - the `mcp_session` registry (list of handed-over sessions);
    - the plugin connect command.
  - Groups 1, 4, 5 and 7 need it landed.
  - Each `## N.` group is one proposed Linear ticket. Its `Carries:` line names the requirements and decisions the group implements. Those requirements' scenarios are the ticket's Done When.
  - ## 1. Read-only session role (`packages/database`)
    - Carries: `agent-read-access` "The database refuses writes from handed-over sessions" and "Handed-over sessions cannot replace the space login"; design Decision 4.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 1.1 Add `mcp_reader` (read-only database role). Grant it to `authenticator` (the role Supabase's REST layer logs in as). Give it `SELECT` on the tables and views retrieval reads. Verify: `pnpm check-schema` passes, and a cucumber scenario shows that `mcp_reader` reads only what the space account can read.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 1.2 Revoke `EXECUTE` on `public` functions from `PUBLIC` (every database role), including the default privileges. Then grant `mcp_reader` `EXECUTE` on an allowlist of the access helpers that policies and views call. Verify:
      - a test lists every function `mcp_reader` can execute, and matches it against the allowlist;
      - the full cucumber suite still passes as `anon` and as `authenticated`;
      - before the revoke, a query on the hosted database shows that `anon` and `authenticated` hold `EXECUTE` on every `public` function (`has_function_privilege`).
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 1.3 Add a custom access token hook (assigns a role at sign-in). When the token's `session_id` is in `mcp_session` (list of handed-over sessions), it sets `role` to `mcp_reader`. Otherwise it returns the claims unchanged. Enable it in `config.toml`, and document how to enable it on the hosted project. Verify: cucumber scenarios for
      - an MCP session getting `mcp_reader`, both on issue and on refresh;
      - a plugin session keeping `authenticated`.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 1.4 Verify that writes are refused. Verify: cucumber scenarios showing that a handed-over session fails to
      - insert, update or delete a node;
      - call `create_secret_token`;
      - call `accept_group_invitation`;
      - call an upsert function.
      - The same writes succeed with the plugin's session.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 1.5 Turn on the space login lock, which stops a session from replacing the space login.
      - Set `secure_password_change` in `config.toml` and on the hosted project.
      - Set "require current password" on the hosted project only, because `config.toml` has no key for it.
      - Verify: an attempt to replace the space login with a session token is refused. Record the result against the hosted project.
  - ## 2. Retrieval functions (`packages/database`)
    - Carries: `discourse-retrieval` "Readable synced collection", "Nodes and types are identified by rid", "Full node text", "Readable type and provenance", "Readable type discovery and filtering" (discovery side) and "One-hop discourse context"; `agent-read-access` "Per-space principal" and "Current permissions"; design Decisions 2, 5 and 7.
    - These functions are verified through the API routes, in the integration tests of task 4.5. So group 2 lands before group 4 or together with it.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 2.1 Add get-node, built on `getSharedNodeByRid` (reads one node by ID) from [ENG-2091](https://linear.app/discourse-graphs/issue/ENG-2091).
      - Fetch the `full` Content (the body) as import does.
      - Add `type` (`rid` and name), `author` (name from `my_accounts`), and `sourceUrl` (Roam) or `sourcePath` (Obsidian).
      - Map `null` to `not_found`.
      - Map a conversion failure, or a missing body, to `content_unavailable`.
      - Verify: task 4.5 covers get-node, and `pnpm check-types` passes.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 2.2 Add type discovery: the types present on readable node instances, identified by type `rid` and labelled with `dbNodeSchemasToCrossApp` (converts node types). Verify: task 4.5 covers type discovery.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 2.3 Add one-hop context.
      - Read the readable relation instances whose `refs` (list of referenced IDs) contain the node.
      - Take direction from `reference_content` (the relation's endpoint map).
      - Drop a relation whose type or endpoint the caller can't read, then convert the rest with `dbRelationsToCrossApp` (converts relations).
      - Verify: task 4.5 covers discourse context.
  - ## 3. Search (`packages/database`)
    - Carries: `discourse-retrieval` "Semantic search over existing embeddings", "Search result fields", "Bounded search without a relevance cutoff" and "Readable type discovery and filtering" (filter side); design Decision 6.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 3.1 Add a `SECURITY INVOKER` (runs as the caller) search RPC. It takes a 1536-dimension vector, optional type `rid`s and a limit. It:
      - joins the current non-obsolete `direct` (title) and `direct_and_description` (title plus description) embeddings of readable node instances;
      - keeps each node's best similarity once;
      - filters and deduplicates before the limit;
      - sorts by similarity, then by `rid`;
      - applies no threshold.
      - Leave `match_content_embeddings` (the existing semantic search query) unchanged. Verify: cucumber scenarios with deterministic vectors for
        - type filtering before the limit;
        - one result per node;
        - a negative similarity that is returned;
        - a node without an embedding, which is absent;
        - unchanged results from `match_content_embeddings`.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 3.2 Add the search function. It calls the RPC, converts hits with `dbNodesToCrossApp` (converts nodes), and adds the same `type`, `author` and source fields as get-node, plus `score` and an excerpt. The excerpt is the first 300 code points of the body, falling back to the title. Verify: unit tests for both excerpt sources and for a multi-byte character at the cut. Task 4.5 covers the result fields.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 3.3 Grant `EXECUTE` on the RPC to `mcp_reader`, and regenerate `dbTypes`. Verify: `pnpm lint`, `pnpm check-types` and `pnpm check-schema` pass.
  - ## 4. Retrieval API (`apps/website`)
    - Carries: `headless-retrieval-api` "Four retrieval operations", "Response envelope", "Authenticated sessions", "Versioned contract" and "Headless retrieval after setup"; the `discourse-retrieval` scenarios, which task 4.5 verifies through the routes; design Decisions 1, 6 and 8.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.1 Add the shared Zod input and output schemas (typed request and response definitions) to `packages/database`, along with the response envelope and the error codes from Decision 8. Verify: `pnpm test:unit` covers
      - a blank query;
      - a malformed `rid`;
      - an empty explicit type list;
      - the default limit of 10, and rejection of limits outside 1-30.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.2 Add a request-scoped auth helper. It validates the session token with Supabase Auth, and builds a client that acts as the caller. Verify: integration tests that
      - a missing, invalid or expired token returns 401;
      - a valid session token returns data.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.3 Add the four `/api/v1` routes over the group 2 and 3 functions, with the HTTP codes from Decision 8. Search embeds the query on the server after authentication, through an OpenAI helper extracted from `/api/embeddings/openai/small`. That route keeps its behavior. Verify: `pnpm test:integration` in `apps/website` covers each route's success and error cases, the envelope on each, and an embedding failure returning 503.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.4 Enforce complete-or-error responses against a 1 MiB budget, and keep logs to the fields in Decision 8. Verify:
      - an integration test for `result_too_large`;
      - a log capture that contains no `Authorization` header, query text or body.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.5 Add retrieval integration tests in `apps/website`. They call the `/api/v1` routes with a handed-over session (`mcp_reader`, the read-only database role), against the local database. A seeding helper writes Roam-style rows (a title, or a title plus description) and Obsidian-style rows (a file-name title, with `filePath` in the title row's metadata). Verify: tests pass for
      - get-node:
        - a readable node;
        - an unreadable node, returning the same `not_found` as a missing one;
        - a missing body;
        - a stored empty body;
        - the type name, author name, and source link or path, on each platform;
        - the returned `rid` (global node ID) matching discovery's;
      - type discovery:
        - types that appear only on readable nodes;
        - two same-named types in different spaces;
      - discourse context:
        - incoming and outgoing relations;
        - an unreadable endpoint, which is excluded;
        - a self-link, in both directions;
        - ordinary backlinks, which are excluded;
      - search: the result fields on each platform;
      - access:
        - a private node in another space isn't returned;
        - a node shared from another space is returned;
        - access gained or lost after handoff applies without a new session.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 4.6 Write an internal API reference: a `README` next to the routes for maintainers of the MCP and of the API. It covers each route's input and output, the response envelope, the error codes and HTTP statuses, session auth, and the rule that `/api/v1` changes only additively. Verify: the PR reviewer checks it against the shared Zod schemas.
  - ## 5. Local MCP (`apps/mcp`, location proposed)
    - Carries: `discourse-mcp` "Tools are thin clients of the API" and "Distribution and setup"; design Decision 9.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 5.1 Scaffold a stdio server on the MCP TypeScript SDK that logs only to stderr. Verify: a test asserts that only protocol messages reach stdout.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 5.2 Add `connect --name <name>`. It reads the bundle from stdin, refreshes once, and checks that the `role` claim is `mcp_reader` (read-only database role). It then writes `~/.discourse-graphs/mcp/<name>.json` with mode 0600. Verify: unit tests for success, a wrong role, and the file mode.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 5.3 On start, the MCP loads the state file, takes a lock, and refuses to run without `mcp_reader`. It writes each rotated refresh token atomically before using it. Verify: unit tests that
      - a refresh writes the new token;
      - a second process exits with a clear error;
      - a wrong role stops startup.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 5.4 Implement the four tools.
      - Each calls one `/api/v1` route with the current session token.
      - Each returns structured content plus a compact text form.
      - Each passes API error codes through as tool errors. An unreachable API is `dependency_error`.
      - Every tool carries a read-only annotation, and its input definition is generated from the shared Zod schema.
      - Verify: per-tool unit tests against a mocked API for
        - success;
        - each error code;
        - an unreachable API.
        - Another test checks that the listed tools are exactly these four.
  - ## 6. Distribution and docs
    - Carries: `discourse-mcp` "Distribution and setup" and "Supported agent clients"; design Decision 9.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 6.1 Publish `@discourse-graphs/mcp` (name proposed) to npm from CI, with semver releases. Verify: a dry-run publish runs in CI, and one published prerelease installs and runs with `npx`.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 6.2 Write the MCP setup README for Claude Code, Claude Desktop and Codex. It covers
      - connect;
      - one bundle and one `--name` per client;
      - the state file risk;
      - search limits, including that search matches titles (and Roam descriptions), not bodies;
      - sync-gap errors;
      - that ChatGPT is unsupported.
      - Verify: someone outside the change follows it in each client.
  - ## 7. End-to-end validation and release
    - Carries: `discourse-mcp` "Supported agent clients"; `headless-retrieval-api` "Headless retrieval after setup"; `agent-read-access` "Session sign-out" and "The database refuses writes from handed-over sessions".
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 7.1 Install the MCP in Claude Desktop, Claude Code and Codex with a test space's bundle. Complete all four operations with Roam and Obsidian closed. Verify: client versions, and one transcript per client, are recorded in the PR.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 7.2 In each client, ask the agent to change a node through a direct Supabase call, using the state file's session. Verify: the database refuses it, and the refusal is recorded in the PR.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 7.3 Sign out the session with local scope, meaning only its own session. Verify: it can't refresh, and MCP and API requests fail once its session token expires.
    - {{[[TODO]]}} 7.4 Release to a limited validation cohort. Verify: cohort members complete setup and run all four operations before a wider release.
  - ## Follow-ups outside this change
    - Archive with `/opsx:archive` once every group has landed.
    - Decide whether one agent should span several spaces.
    - Hybrid search, adapting Supabase's recipe (design Decision 6).

tools.description:
Open the Browser pane: pass `url` to open a browser tab at a URL (no dev server needed — use this for external sites, staging, docs, or your deployed app), OR pass `name` to start a dev server from .claude/launch.json.

Start a dev server by name from .claude/launch.json. If .claude/launch.json doesn't exist, create it first with this format:
{
  "version": "0.0.1",
  "configurations": [
    {
      "name": "<unique-name>",
      "runtimeExecutable": "<command>",
      "runtimeArgs": ["<args>"],
      "port": <port>
    }
  ]
}
Set "runtimeExecutable" to the command (e.g. "npm"), "runtimeArgs" to the arguments (e.g. ["run", "dev"]), and "port" to the server port. An optional "url" (http/https) opens the preview there instead of http://localhost:<port>. A localhost "url" must be just the server's origin — no path or query, matching the entry's port — for example "https://localhost:8443" or "http://app.localhost:3000"; to show a specific page, navigate after the preview opens. Non-localhost URLs may carry paths and are subject to the user's permission and the organization's browsing policy. A configuration with "url" and no command attaches to an already-running server. Only include servers you actually need to preview. Reuses the server if already running. ALWAYS use this instead of Bash for running servers. If the deliverable is already published as an Artifact, update the Artifact instead of starting a server to show it.

